273
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:53,480
Next, it's not just about blowing things up. Demolition has a crush on recycling.

274
00:19:55,480 --> 00:20:01,480
The work of demolition, until around the middle of the 20th century, was mostly manual labor.

275
00:20:01,481 --> 00:20:06,480
But after World War II, technology took on the heavy work.

276
00:20:09,480 --> 00:20:14,480
There was another development after the war that had a massive impact on the demolition industry.

277
00:20:14,481 --> 00:20:16,018
industry, recycling.

278
00:20:16,019 --> 00:20:21,480
There was a high demand for metals, steel, iron, tin, copper, aluminum,

279
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:26,696
and it cost less to reuse scrap than to mine new ore.

280
00:20:26,697 --> 00:20:29,480
Suddenly, there was new money to be

281
00:20:29,481 --> 00:20:31,480
made from old junk.

282
00:20:31,481 --> 00:20:37,480
Right now in this country, estimates are that about 135 million tons of demolition

283
00:20:37,481 --> 00:20:38,968
debris are generated every year.

284
00:20:38,969 --> 00:20:41,480
The volume of that material that is recycled continues

285
00:20:41,481 --> 00:20:46,380
continues to increase. Estimates are it's up to about 40% of the total volume.

286
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:55,560
Anything from run-down refrigerators to rusty pipes, file cabinets, water heaters, pots and pans,

287
00:20:55,960 --> 00:21:02,660
tricycles and tin cans, any metal that anybody wants moved can make its way to places like this.

288
00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:07,160
A1 Metal Recycling in Southern California.

289
00:21:07,161 --> 00:21:08,999
A1 is a busy place.

290
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:19,160
Ben Wilcox is sort of a combination traffic cop and field commander, directing a steady stream of trucks.

291
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:27,840
Right now the yard is taking in tons of structural steel from the demolition of a Los Angeles landmark.

292
00:21:28,900 --> 00:21:34,320
The Sherman Oaks Galleria being gutted for conversion from a mall into office space.

293
00:21:34,321 --> 00:21:41,980
And so the rebar and these cut-up pieces of flooring you see in the background all came off of the galleria.

294
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:44,360
It's a big part of what's going on right now.

295
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:53,720
Workers called burners use acetylene torches to slice the spaghetti-like tangle of rebar into manageable lengths.

296
00:21:54,480 --> 00:22:03,020
Then, like a hungry vulture, the claw swoops in and snatches up 1,000-pound bites, swinging them into the waiting trucks.

297
00:22:03,021 --> 00:22:12,200
The yard is in constant motion, where everyone knows time is money, and the mission is move them in and move them out.

298
00:22:12,840 --> 00:22:14,940
The machines are what make that possible.

299
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:20,280
The mag is another workhorse that gathers up the ferrous or magnetic material.

300
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:27,900
It's got a 48-inch electromagnet on it, and we use it to pick up the steel and pick it up off the ground,

301
00:22:27,901 --> 00:22:31,780
loaded in a truck, pick it up off the ground as it's dumped out of the little trucks, and

302
00:22:31,781 --> 00:22:34,940
put it up into a big pile so we don't just spread it out all over the yard.

303
00:22:35,540 --> 00:22:41,180
These men make it look easy, but operating these mammoth machines in a crowded yard on

304
00:22:41,181 --> 00:22:44,140
a tight schedule takes considerable skill.

305
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:49,400
It's certainly moving in many directions at once, with the jaw opening and closing and

306
00:22:49,401 --> 00:22:54,720
the boom moving in two places, and it's swinging, and you control all those movements simultaneously

307
00:22:54,721 --> 00:22:57,000
and make all those movements simultaneously.

308
00:22:57,001 --> 00:22:59,000
So there is a lot going on.

309
00:22:59,001 --> 00:23:04,000
A1 also processes a limited number of cars,

310
00:23:04,001 --> 00:23:08,000
but process is perhaps too mild a turn.

311
00:23:08,001 --> 00:23:15,000
Along with the wrecking ball, the other most ominous implement of demolition has to be the crusher.

312
00:23:15,001 --> 00:23:20,000
You put the car in and it takes and smashes it down to just about a foot high.

313
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
The purpose of that is just for transportation.

314
00:23:24,001 --> 00:23:24,701
transportation.

315
00:23:24,702 --> 00:23:27,820
You get about six cars on a truck if you don't smash them, and if you

316
00:23:27,821 --> 00:23:31,000
smash them, we get about 18 of them.

317
00:23:31,001 --> 00:23:37,022
Move them in, move them out. That's the name of the recycling game.

318
00:23:37,023 --> 00:23:39,000
And the more you move,

319
00:23:39,001 --> 00:23:40,400
the more you make.

320
00:23:40,401 --> 00:23:46,000
And this is just one stop along the road to renewal for these jam-packed

321
00:23:46,001 --> 00:23:47,193
loads of metal.

322
00:23:47,194 --> 00:23:53,000
From here, the loaded trucks lumber south to A1's parent company, Adelio,

323
00:23:53,001 --> 00:23:54,727
parent company, Adam Steel.

324
00:23:54,728 --> 00:23:58,500
Adam Steel is one of the largest steel recycling facilities

325
00:23:58,501 --> 00:23:59,612
in the country.

326
00:23:59,613 --> 00:24:05,100
The mill is the heart of Adam's recycling operation, or you could think of

327
00:24:05,101 --> 00:24:11,680
it as the ultimate cast iron stomach. The operator of this grapple feeds it a steady

328
00:24:11,681 --> 00:24:12,953
diet of metal.

329
00:24:12,954 --> 00:24:19,680
The grapple places scrap metal onto what's called the infeed belt, hauling

330
00:24:19,681 --> 00:24:25,520
the scrap up to the top of the mill where gravity helps deliver it into what has to be the ultimate

331
00:24:25,521 --> 00:24:28,480
chamber of horrors, the shredder.

332
00:24:28,481 --> 00:24:34,040
The shredder box is a large magnesium box, approximately eight

333
00:24:34,041 --> 00:24:35,341
foot square.

334
00:24:35,342 --> 00:24:44,120
Inside this shredder box, there's 34, 375 pound magnesium hammers swinging at 200

335
00:24:44,121 --> 00:24:47,642
miles an hour and they don't stop for anything.

336
00:24:47,643 --> 00:24:50,940
The shredder is a masterpiece of destructive

337
00:24:50,941 --> 00:24:51,754
ingenuity.

338
00:24:51,755 --> 00:24:59,000
It takes just 45 seconds for an entire car, engine and all, to be pummeled into thousands

339
00:24:59,001 --> 00:25:02,597
of pieces, no larger than a grapefruit.

340
00:25:02,598 --> 00:25:06,840
The material now known as shreds rides another

341
00:25:06,841 --> 00:25:12,064
conveyor along what could be considered an assembly line in reverse.

342
00:25:12,065 --> 00:25:13,600
Magnets sort out the

343
00:25:13,601 --> 00:25:20,280
ferrous material which then pass along to a shed where sharp-eyed pickers give it one last going

344
00:25:20,281 --> 00:25:20,981
over.

345
00:25:20,982 --> 00:25:25,856
The material you'll see that what the pickers are pulling off is parts of car tires.

346
00:25:25,857 --> 00:25:26,557
The steel

347
00:25:26,558 --> 00:25:31,672
belts will stick with the magnets but we don't want the rubber part of the tire.

348
00:25:31,673 --> 00:25:32,460
They'll pull

349
00:25:32,461 --> 00:25:37,886
off the copper coring from motors. The copper becomes magnetized inside of motors.

350
00:25:37,887 --> 00:25:38,680
They'll pull

351
00:25:38,681 --> 00:25:43,160
that out or any other type of trash they got pulled along with the metal that didn't get

352
00:25:43,161 --> 00:25:44,900
separated by the magnetic separators.

353
00:25:46,360 --> 00:25:52,140
Finally, the mashed, sheared, shredded and sorted bits drop into a pile.

354
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:55,980
This is Adam's final product.

355
00:25:57,200 --> 00:26:00,320
From here, the shreds are shipped to mills,

356
00:26:00,660 --> 00:26:04,060
where they are melted down and cast into new products,

357
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:07,740
such as rebar, rolled steel, or wire.

358
00:26:07,741 --> 00:26:13,340
New metal from old

359
00:26:13,341 --> 00:26:17,720
And what once was waste is now a new resource

360
00:26:17,721 --> 00:26:21,400
But it is not far-fetched to imagine down the line

