The charges have been checked and rechecked.

475
00:35:32,500 --> 00:35:34,840
The cavernous building is ready for its demise.

476
00:35:35,380 --> 00:35:37,020
All traffic has been stopped.

477
00:35:37,560 --> 00:35:39,100
Trains are on hold.

478
00:35:40,960 --> 00:35:42,500
The alert sounds.

479
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:45,740
Cameras roll.

480
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:51,740
And the MCM team can only watch and hope nobody missed anything.

481
00:36:02,080 --> 00:36:03,580
Right where we want it.

482
00:36:04,860 --> 00:36:05,920
Yeah, baby!

483
00:36:06,540 --> 00:36:07,240
Yeah, baby!

484
00:36:08,180 --> 00:36:09,600
Nice job, baby.

485
00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:13,100
It went exactly as planned.

486
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:15,160
An extraordinary accomplishment.

487
00:36:16,420 --> 00:36:22,280
When the dust settles, a few of the MCM crew explore the aftermath.

488
00:36:23,660 --> 00:36:29,260
The top of the tower lands just where it was supposed to, well short of the safety margin.

489
00:36:30,280 --> 00:36:33,620
The torch cuts and explosive charges work flawlessly.

490
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:39,980
As you can see here, this is what your safe charge does right here.

491
00:36:40,060 --> 00:36:41,444
This was not cut with the torch.

492
00:36:41,445 --> 00:36:43,780
This is completely cut with an electrical safe charge.

493
00:36:43,781 --> 00:36:46,320
Pettigrew's planning paid off.

494
00:36:46,920 --> 00:36:49,980
Not only did he drop the building exactly where he said he would,

495
00:36:50,340 --> 00:36:53,860
but the cutting of key crossbeams caused it to flatten out,

496
00:36:54,560 --> 00:37:00,860
making the task of sorting and salvaging far safer, easier, and economical for MCM.

497
00:37:02,200 --> 00:37:09,020
We probably put 1,200 tons of building material on the ground in a very short period of time.

498
00:37:09,021 --> 00:37:13,640
We did it safely. There was no property damage. There were no injuries.

499
00:37:13,780 --> 00:37:16,880
and we don't expect either.

500
00:37:17,940 --> 00:37:19,440
It's nice when it happens.

501
00:37:20,340 --> 00:37:21,380
It's nice that it's over.

502
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:26,620
We'll start tomorrow morning with the separation of building materials

503
00:37:26,621 --> 00:37:31,720
and we will prepare and transport and recycle

504
00:37:31,721 --> 00:37:38,060
probably 98% of the building material from the structure.

505
00:37:38,600 --> 00:37:43,460
There really is something almost mythological about this work.

506
00:37:43,780 --> 00:37:47,840
Something deep, inexplicable, irresistible.

507
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:49,780
Demolition is life.

508
00:37:51,060 --> 00:37:54,280
It's the bomb. It's really the best job I've ever had.

509
00:37:54,700 --> 00:38:02,460
I done worked in factories, hung fenders, shook out motors, machine shops, stamping plants.

510
00:38:02,780 --> 00:38:04,720
But I've always come back to demolition.

511
00:38:05,860 --> 00:38:10,240
Next, see how demolition contractors save lives.

512
00:38:10,241 --> 00:38:21,328
Perhaps no other event in American history more clearly defines what demolition is and what it is not than the bombing of the Frederick J.

513
00:38:21,329 --> 00:38:23,980
Murrah building in Oklahoma City.

514
00:38:25,060 --> 00:38:29,780
Demolition is not what Timothy McVeigh did, though he surely demolished a building.

515
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:34,220
Demolition is what several Midwest demolition companies did,

516
00:38:34,640 --> 00:38:37,000
which was to speed their equipment to the site,

517
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:40,100
put their considerable knowledge and skill to work,

518
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:43,560
and make a major contribution to the rescue effort.

519
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:48,440
Ben Cates was one of the first contractors at the Murrah Building in Oklahoma City.

520
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:54,020
He began working with the local officials there, the police department, fire department,

521
00:38:54,240 --> 00:38:59,440
to assess the damage and determine if they could rescue anybody out of that facility.

522
00:38:59,441 --> 00:39:01,980
His equipment was on site to begin that effort.

523
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:09,560
And then as they decided to clear the individual floors, to remove the bodies, to investigate the crime scene,

524
00:39:09,980 --> 00:39:14,640
the demolition contractors, excavators, cranes were there involved directly with that work.

525
00:39:15,480 --> 00:39:21,440
Demolition contractors have been there on the countless occasions when disaster struck.

526
00:39:21,840 --> 00:39:24,580
In rescue situations, in earthquakes and other natural disasters,

527
00:39:24,581 --> 00:39:30,040
Oftentimes a demolition contractor will bring his excavator to lift some of the debris out of the way

528
00:39:30,041 --> 00:39:33,580
so that the rescuers can try to find survivors under that debris.

529
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:36,260
New challenges lay ahead, too.

530
00:39:36,700 --> 00:39:41,960
Environmental hazards and risks that have yet to be confronted head-on by society.

531
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:46,760
The cleanup of hazardous waste sites has been a source of work for the demolition industry for many years.

532
00:39:46,761 --> 00:39:56,700
and now the government has developed a program to take what may amount to as many as 450,000 contaminated sites in this country.

533
00:39:56,701 --> 00:40:01,320
It can be everything from a gas station to an old dry cleaning facility that need to be cleaned up.

534
00:40:01,720 --> 00:40:06,900
That's the work of demolition contractors, the soil remediation, the general facilities decontamination work.

535
00:40:07,220 --> 00:40:08,640
Demolition contractors do that work.

536
00:40:09,620 --> 00:40:16,200
There are already a raft of safety regulations to protect demolition workers from hazardous materials.

537
00:40:16,760 --> 00:40:22,460
But nobody has yet figured out how the most hazardous material of all will have to be handled.

538
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:27,800
In the United States right now, there are 108 nuclear power plants.

539
00:40:28,500 --> 00:40:33,720
A lot of those plants are reaching the end of their useful life before recertification.

540
00:40:34,160 --> 00:40:38,320
And many of the electrical utilities may decide they don't want to recertify the plant

541
00:40:38,321 --> 00:40:41,940
because it costs almost as much to recertify as it does to build a new plant.

542
00:40:41,941 --> 00:40:47,060
the demolition removal of those plants, the nuclear and non-nuclear aspects of those plants,

543
00:40:47,140 --> 00:40:49,240
is going to be done by the demolition industry in this country.

544
00:40:51,580 --> 00:40:57,320
Demolition companies are also moving rapidly to the front lines of another environmental battle,

545
00:40:57,800 --> 00:41:01,440
the removal of potentially hundreds of dams throughout the country.

546
00:41:02,320 --> 00:41:06,420
More than 75,000 dams have been built on American rivers

547
00:41:06,421 --> 00:41:10,180
for reasons including water storage and electricity generation,

548
00:41:10,181 --> 00:41:14,360
many of them over the passionate protests of environmentalists.

549
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:16,860
Now, the tide may be turning.

550
00:41:17,940 --> 00:41:22,540
The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission is refusing to re-license dams,

551
00:41:22,820 --> 00:41:26,980
where environmental damage outweighs the benefits of hydroelectric power,

552
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:31,920
which may unleash a torrent of dramatic demolitions.

553
00:41:32,880 --> 00:41:36,020
Demolition companies may become dam busters.

554
00:41:36,021 --> 00:41:44,120
The first dam to come down under the new order was Maine's Edwards Dam on the Kennebec River in July 1999.

555
00:41:45,920 --> 00:41:48,000
It was a simple enough job.

556
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:56,740
A backhoe weakened the underpinnings of the gravel dam, and then a final notch in the concrete cap, and the river did the rest.

557
00:41:59,120 --> 00:42:04,020
Some demolition experts are even becoming more and more like their historic nemeses,

558
00:42:04,021 --> 00:42:06,659
nemeses, the preservationists.

559
00:42:06,660 --> 00:42:11,760
The industry also salvages beautiful architectural pieces.

560
00:42:12,360 --> 00:42:17,917
The reuse of this material is a constant part of the things that the industry does.

561
00:42:17,918 --> 00:42:18,720
Jim Canfield

562
00:42:18,721 --> 00:42:21,866
is both a demolition contractor and a preservationist.

563
00:42:21,867 --> 00:42:24,720
They're going to tear a house down to the ground.

564
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:30,100
I usually step in, they give me a week or whatever before the bulldozers come, and I just go in and

565
00:42:30,101 --> 00:42:31,418
strip everything out.

566
00:42:31,419 --> 00:42:36,000
I'll take the hinges, the doorknob, everything, and I take it apart piece

567
00:42:36,001 --> 00:42:39,579
by piece slowly so I don't break anything, and I save every screw.

568
00:42:39,580 --> 00:42:40,880
Everything goes with it,

569
00:42:41,220 --> 00:42:43,921
and it even goes down to the old two-by-fours.

570
00:42:43,922 --> 00:42:46,680
The old rough wood they used a long time ago is

571
00:42:46,681 --> 00:42:51,640
still strong and good today as it was back in 50, 60 years ago or more, and there's people out there

572
00:42:51,641 --> 00:42:52,341
that buy it.

573
00:42:52,342 --> 00:42:56,060
By saving it and salvaging, I'm keeping history going, keeping the past alive.

574
00:42:56,061 --> 00:43:02,680
How long will it be before demolition companies take their talents aloft?

575
00:43:03,340 --> 00:43:06,100
The economic incentive is already there.

576
00:43:06,740 --> 00:43:10,840
With billions of dollars worth of scientific refuse circling the globe,

577
00:43:11,320 --> 00:43:15,440
with their mechanical marvels already performing amazing feats on Earth,

578
00:43:15,900 --> 00:43:21,080
imagine what an ultra, ultra high demolition tool could do in zero gravity.

579
00:43:21,800 --> 00:43:23,560
We don't know where it's all going to take us.

580
00:43:23,561 --> 00:43:30,460
It's possible that we may, 20, 30, 40 years from now, be involved with the demolition of the space station,

581
00:43:30,760 --> 00:43:35,200
the recycling of the elements of the space station that's being built now in outer space.

582
00:43:35,320 --> 00:43:37,100
We're working on the moon. We certainly hope so.

583
00:43:37,860 --> 00:43:44,960
As it was in ancient times and more than ever today, whatever man builds, man can destroy.

584
00:43:44,961 --> 00:43:53,940
Only today, the task is to reclaim and reuse the pieces of the past that have value for the future.

585
00:43:54,940 --> 00:43:58,660
And the delicate art of demolition will always be there.

586
00:43:59,040 --> 00:44:08,720
One step behind the builders, removing the rubble of the past, reclaiming, recycling, and clearing the way for whatever that future may hold.

